5,"team":"LA","distance":0.7807,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Chris Shula"},{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.784,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"NO","distance":0.8383,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2025,"team":"WAS","distance":0.8473,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Titans went 7-10 in 2022 and were outscored 359 to 298. The offense ranked 25th in EPA per play and the defense 18th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 13 personnel usage and the 2nd highest dime usage in the league.","offense":"The Titans offense was well below average by EPA per play. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 13 personnel usage and the 2nd most tight ends on the field in the league. Ryan Tannehill ran the passing game, with Robert Woods leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Titans defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 2nd and 28th. Denico Autry was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was DeMarcus Walker, who had 7 sacks while playing only 36% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest dime usage in the league.","special":"The Titans were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 83% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 21% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 52% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Ryan Tannehill played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,536 yards. Targets were spread around, with Robert Woods and Austin Hooper leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Derrick Henry handled most of the work in the backfield with 83% of the carries. On defense, Denico Autry led the team with 8 sacks and Kevin Byard picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Steelers and the 2022 Cowboys under Kellen Moore. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Packers and the 2025 Rams under Chris Shula.","trends":"The offense went from 15th in EPA per play in 2021 to 25th in 2022, a big step back. The defense moved from 9th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 18th in 2022, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 47% to 23%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}